# Commit messages

Write commit messages as **Trevor describing what he changed** — for a reader on GitHub, not as instructions to Trevor.

## Voice

- First person / author perspective: what was added, updated, or fixed.
- Prefer clear, specific summaries of the actual diff.
- Gerund or past-tense narration is fine (`Adding…`, `Updated…`, `Describing…`) when it reads naturally.
- Imperative mood is also fine when it stays specific (`Add gitleaks CI…`).

## Never use

Placeholder or second-person template text — these must never become the commit subject:

- `Your message describing the changes`
- `Describe your change in one clear sentence.`
- `Short description of what changed`
- `Describe your Pick Six / share updates`
- Any variant of “your message”, “describe your…”, or “short description of…”

If the diff is not summarized yet, **write the real summary** from the staged changes. Do not commit with placeholder text.

## Examples

| Bad (placeholder / second person) | Good (author describing the work) |
| --- | --- |
| Your message describing the changes | Added Pick Six live scoring, fantasy depth chart, and NFL team context |
| Describe your change in one clear sentence. | Expanded player detail dialog and 2026 strength-of-schedule data |
| Short description of what changed | Added rookies to the 2026 draft board CSV |
| Describe your Pick Six / share updates | Updated Pick Six share card, jersey assets, and rankings ramp-up |

When committing on the user’s behalf, always draft the subject from `git diff` / `git status`, in this voice.
